{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target answers both parts of the problem clearly: it identifies the mechanism of stored sensible heat released after sunset as the reason for the city being warmer, and it identifies built density and impervious surface fraction as the specific factors causing the gap to be larger in some cities than in others.","premise":"The target explains why a city stays warmer than the surrounding countryside on a clear, calm night and what single mechanism best explains the cross-city variation in that nighttime gap.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"5e730cf04d9ad2b3","quote":"The night-time UHI is dominated by stored sensible heat: urban masonry and asphalt absorb daytime solar radiation and, having high thermal mass and heat capacity, release that stored energy as longwave radiation after sunset, slowing the urban cooling rate relative to the countryside. The cross-city magnitude of this gap scales with built density and impervious surface fraction, as denser, more massive urban fabrics retain more heat per unit area."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}